(a) Appointment and powers. The Chief of Police shall be appointed by the Mayor, by and with the advice and consent of City Council, and shall be the department head for the Police Department, subject always to the orders and regulations of the Mayor and City Council and reporting directly to the City Manager with regard to day-to-day operations. In the exercise of his duties, the Chief of Police shall have the power to establish and enforce rules and regulations for the government of the members and employees of the Police Department, which rules shall, however, be consistent with the ordinances of the City and the laws of the State.
(b) Oath. Prior to entering upon the discharge of his duties, the Chief of Police shall take and subscribe the oath prescribed by the Mayor and Council.
(c) Duties. The Chief of Police shall have the following duties:
(1) To attend all meetings of the City Council;
(2) To see to the proper service of all summonses, subpoenas, citations, executions, attachments and rules of the City Council;
(3) To see to the collection of all fines and costs imposed by the Municipal Court;
(4) To see that the ordinances, rules and regulations of the City and all statutes applicable therein are faithfully enforced;
(5) To establish rules and regulations concerning the discipline, good order, proper conduct, care, and management of the Police Department, subject to approval by the City Council;
(6) To preserve the public peace, prevent infractions of the law, and arrest violators thereof;
(7) To protect the rights of persons and property;
(8) To oversee the conduct of the officers and members of the Police Department, and to be held strictly responsible for such conduct and for the general good order of the Police Department;
(9) To keep adequate records of all personnel and equipment of the Police Department including a log of all questions, activities and investigations;
(10) To submit to the State or Federal authorities any report as may be required by State of Federal law; and
(11) To discharge such other duties as may be required of him by the Mayor and City Council or City Manager.
(d) Resignation, removal, death, or disability. In the event of resignation, removal, death, or disability of the Chief of Police, the Mayor and City Council shall appoint an acting Chief of Police who shall perform the duties of the Chief until removal of such disability or until a successor has been duly appointed.
(Code 1978, § 2-322)
